cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it and the article it accompanies."
title: "MayaBRIC: structure-level salt-trade resilience scores for detected Maya structures"
version: 1.0.0
date-released: 2026-11-02
license: MIT
abstract: |
  What this is
  MayaBRIC gives every ancient Maya structure on a map a score from 0 to 1. The score describes how well the place was connected to salt and to the political centres that organised its trade. This record holds the code that computes the score, the settings it uses, and the locations of the twelve salt sources it measures against.

  Why it exists
  Salt was a daily necessity for the ancient Maya, for diet and for preserving food. Most of it came from a few places, mainly on the coast, but many people lived far inland and depended on trade to get it. A household close to salt and to several large centres had more ways to get salt than one far from both, so it was less exposed when one route or one source failed. Airborne LiDAR now shows tens of thousands of buildings that were never on any map. In our study, a deep-learning model found 57,181 of them on the Yucatán Peninsula. MayaBRIC applies the same open, repeatable measure to every one of them. Researchers can then compare places across the whole region instead of one site at a time. Anyone can also check the published numbers, because this code reproduces them.

  What it does
  For any point on the map, it asks three questions:
  1. How far is the nearest salt? It works out the travel time, in hours, on foot or by canoe from the point to the cheapest of the twelve salt sources. Travel accounts for hills, rivers, the coast and water that can't be crossed.
  2. How many important centres are nearby? It counts the ranked sites within 25 km, about a day's round trip on foot, and gives larger centres more weight.
  3. How strongly is the point tied to a major centre? It measures the strongest pull from one nearby centre, which is stronger when the centre is larger and closer.
  Each answer becomes a score between 0 and 1. MayaBRIC is the average of the three, and the result is sorted into four bands, 0 to 3. The code also groups the structures into site-level clusters. It flags clusters with no recorded site within 10 km, because these may be settlements that have not been recorded.

  Who it's for
  Archaeologists and GIS researchers who want to use or extend the scores, and reviewers or readers who want to check how the published catalog was made.

  How to use it
  - Score your own points: start with any table of point locations (latitude and longitude) and run the three steps in the README to get travel time to salt, the three pillar scores, the MayaBRIC score and band, and the clusters.
  - Score a single named site: scripts/score_site.py takes a name and coordinates and returns that site's scores. For example, Calakmul scores 0.591.
  - Check the published catalog: tests/verify_against_catalog.py recomputes every score and compares it with the catalog.

  What you need, and what isn't here
  Some inputs can't be shared in this record. The locations of the 57,181 structures are restricted to protect the sites from looting. Identified researchers can get them from the corresponding author. The ranked site list comes from the Electronic Atlas of Ancient Maya Sites and is available from its authors. The travel-cost map is built from free public data, but the script that builds it is not included yet. Until it is, its settings are recorded in config/friction_config.yaml. docs/INPUTS.md lists every input and where to get it.

  Full documentation and the latest code: https://github.com/benjbritton/MayaBRIC
